{"name":"Double Click","hex":"#d0d2d1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"double click","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of double-click."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/double_click"}],"entries":[{"title":"Double-click","blocks":[{"text":"A double-click is the act of pressing a computer mouse button twice quickly without moving the mouse. Double-clicking allows two different actions to be associated with the same mouse button. It was developed by Tim Mott of Xerox Palo Alto Research Center. Often, single-clicking selects an object while a double-click selects the next object up in the selection hierarchy, or executes the function associated with that object. Following a link in a modern web browser is accomplished with only a single click, requiring the use of a second mouse button, \"click and hold\" delay, or modifier key to gain access to actions other than following the link. On touchscreens, the double-click is called \"double-tap\"; it's not used as much as double-click, but typically it functions as a zoom feature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Gestures_Double_Tap.png/330px-Gestures_Double_Tap.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double-click","wikidata":"Q1369585"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Gestures_Double_Tap.png/500px-Gestures_Double_Tap.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Gestures_Double_Tap.png/500px-Gestures_Double_Tap.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"double-click","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGestures_Double_Tap.png"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Werner's nomenclature of colours greys","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu